{
  "image_name": "3cfbf587e801ba87b21b92123dbecd33.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a signaling network that links cell-surface receptors to activation of ERM proteins and, ultimately, actin crosslinking at the plasma membrane. Signals begin at either a GPCR or an antigen/tyrosine kinase receptor, which then stimulate several small GTPases through GEF-mediated GDP-to-GTP exchange. The main GTPases shown are RAP1, RHOA, and RAC1, each switching from an inactive GDP-bound form to an active conformation when GTP-bound.\n\nThese activated GTPases feed into different downstream effectors. RAP1 and RHOA converge on kinases labeled SLK and STK10, while RHOA also signals through ROCK1 or ROCK2. RAC1 activates pathways involving VAV1, TAOK3, and a protein labeled KIAA0355, with one branch appearing less certain, indicated by question marks. Another uncertain connection is shown between LCK and TAOK3 near the receptor.\n\nAll of these branches converge on ERM proteins, depicted as changing from a closed, inactive form to an open, phosphorylated active form. The active ERM state then promotes actin crosslinking. Overall, the image emphasizes that multiple receptor-triggered GTPase pathways cooperate to control ERM activation and cytoskeletal organization beneath the membrane.",
